{#
  VTEP addresses for EVPN.

  OVN-Kubernetes discovers each node's VXLAN tunnel endpoint address from
  the addresses already on it that fall inside these CIDRs, and annotates the
  node with what it found. The fabric uses those addresses as the next hop
  for every EVPN route, so they have to be routable from the fabric - which
  they are, because the nodes advertise {{ udn_bgp_vtep_prefix }}.0/24 over
  the unicast half of the EVPN session and leaf1 carries a static /32 per
  node besides.

  Deliberately a block that exists nowhere else in this lab: not
  {{ lab_network_prefix }}.0/24, not {{ clab_fabric_prefix }}.0/24, not any
  tenant prefix. A VTEP address that collides with a real one produces
  tunnels that form and then carry traffic to the wrong place.

  mode is 'Unmanaged' - capitalised; the API enum is Managed/Unmanaged.
  Unmanaged means something external assigns each node's address - the
  NNCPs this role renders - and OVN-Kubernetes discovers what is there.

  DO NOT DROP THE mode LINE. It defaults to Managed, and Managed - where
  OVN-Kubernetes would allocate the addresses itself - is not implemented as
  of OpenShift 4.22 and 4.23: the CR comes up Accepted=False, reason
  ManagedModeNotSupported, and ovnkube-node deletes its EVPN devices.
  Source lines and a re-check command are in real-fabric.md section 6.

  Unmanaged suits this lab on its own merits too: the fabric is three FRR
  containers with no IGP, so leaf1 needs a static /32 per VTEP, and assigning
  the addresses here means those routes are written at render time. One less
  moving part when a tunnel does not form.

  Exactly one address per node may fall inside these CIDRs. If a node has two
  the VTEP goes to a failed status rather than picking one - so if you widen
  this block, check nothing else on the nodes is already inside it.

#}
apiVersion: k8s.ovn.org/v1
kind: VTEP
metadata:
  name: evpn-vtep
spec:
  cidrs:
    - {{ udn_bgp_vtep_prefix }}.0/24
  mode: Unmanaged
